Here’s a **complete Java program** that demonstrates a simple **to-do list manager**. This example includes user input, arrays, loops, and basic OOP concepts. You can copy and run it in your IDE or terminal.

---

### 📝 **To-Do List Manager in Java**
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ToDoList {
    // Class-level variables
    private static final Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    private static String[] tasks = new String[10]; // Max 10 tasks
    private static int taskCount = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean running = true;
        while (running) {
            displayMenu();
            int choice = getUserChoice();

            switch (choice) {
                case 1:
                    addTask();
                    break;
                case 2:
                    viewTasks();
                    break;
                case 3:
                    deleteTask();
                    break;
                case 4:
                    running = false;
                    System.out.println("Exiting... Have a great day! 😊");
                    break;
                default:
                    System.out.println("Invalid choice. Try again.");
            }
        }
    }

    // Display the menu
    private static void displayMenu() {
        System.out.println("\n=== To-Do List Manager ===");
        System.out.println("1. Add Task");
        System.out.println("2. View Tasks");
        System.out.println("3. Delete Task");
        System.out.println("4. Exit");
        System.out.print("Choose an option (1-4): ");
    }

    // Get user input
    private static int getUserChoice() {
        while (true) {
            if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
                int choice = scanner.nextInt();
                scanner.nextLine(); // Consume newline
                return choice;
            } else {
                System.out.println("Please enter a valid number.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // Clear invalid input
            }
        }
    }

    // Add a task
    private static void addTask() {
        if (taskCount >= tasks.length) {
            System.out.println("Maximum tasks reached (10). Cannot add more.");
            return;
        }
        System.out.print("Enter task description: ");
        String task = scanner.nextLine();
        tasks[taskCount++] = task;
        System.out.println("Task added successfully!");
    }

    // View all tasks
    private static void viewTasks() {
        if (taskCount == 0) {
            System.out.println("No tasks in the list.");
            return;
        }
        System.out.println("\n=== Your Tasks ===");
        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + tasks[i]);
        }
    }

    // Delete a task
    private static void deleteTask() {
        if (taskCount == 0) {
            System.out.println("No tasks to delete.");
            return;
        }
        viewTasks();
        System.out.print("Enter task number to delete: ");
        int index = scanner.nextInt() - 1;
        scanner.nextLine(); // Consume newline

        if (index >= 0 && index < taskCount) {
            for (int i = index; i < taskCount - 1; i++) {
                tasks[i] = tasks[i + 1];
            }
            taskCount--;
            System.out.println("Task deleted successfully!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid task number.");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 🚀 How to Run This Code
1. **Save** the code in a file named `ToDoList.java`.
2. **Compile** with:  
   ```bash
   javac ToDoList.java
   ```
3. **Run** with:  
   ```bash
   java ToDoList
   ```

---

### 🧠 What This Code Demonstrates
- **Arrays** for storing tasks.
- **Loops** and **conditionals** for menu navigation.
- **OOP principles** (encapsulation, methods).
- **User input/output** using `Scanner`.

